<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alan I, King of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/907">907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Alan the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alan I, called the Great, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> and from about 890 king of Brittany. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> to a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> at Questembert in 888 and reunited the country after a period of division. He was the last Breton ruler to bear the title of king; after his death the Vikings overran Brittany.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alan was the second son of Count Ridoredh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>. He succeeded his brother Pascweten as count of Vannes when Pascweten died, probably in the middle of 876, and then contended for the leadership of Brittany with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicael">Judicael</a> of Poher. Alan stood for the power bloc of south-eastern Brittany and Judicael for western Breton interests, until the two made peace to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>. Judicael died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-questembert">Battle of Questembert</a>, after which Alan held the leadership of Brittany alone. In 890 he defeated the Vikings at Saint-Lô, chasing them into a river where many drowned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alan ruled all of Brittany as it had been under Salomon: the Breton lands of Léon, Domnonée, Cornouaille and the Vannetais, the Frankish counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coutances">Coutances</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a>, and the western parts of Poitou and Anjou, as far east as the river Vire. He was the first Breton ruler to hold all this without great opposition in the west and the last to rule the whole bloc. His strongest rival was Fulk I of Anjou, who disputed the Nantais with him, though Alan seems to have kept the upper hand.</p><p>A charter of between 897 and 900 records prayers Alan ordered at the monastery of Redon for the soul of a Karolus, probably the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, who is thought to have granted him the right to the title of king. Charles had contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> in 886 and sought to bind former enemies to the empire. Alan used Carolingian regalia and Carolingian forms in his charters, and his power grew under the weak reigns of Odo and Charles III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alan died in 907, and Count Gourmaëlon of Cornouaille seized power in a disputed succession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> soon overran Brittany, killed Gourmaëlon in 913/914 and held the land until 936, when Alan's grandson Alan II returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>'s England. Brittany never again reached the extent it had under Alan I. With his wife Oreguen he had several children, among them Pascweten, Guerec, Budic and Rudalt, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>; an 11th-century genealogy makes him an ancestor of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alan I of Brittany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Salomon’s murder in 874 and the death of <b>Alan the Great</b> around 907, Viking raids intensified, and in 919 a Norse army overran the country; monks fled with their relics and many nobles took refuge in England.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After internal conflict <b>Alan the Great</b> defeated the Vikings at Questembert around 888, but after his death in 907 Vikings overran Brittany.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Rieux, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.6,-2.11&amp;year=897" aria-label="Rieux on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Alan I, King of Brittany"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4879 6050h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4879 6050h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.6,-2.11&amp;year=897">What was here in 897 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
